  "answer_text": "The word **comet** has both a scientific meaning and a fascinating linguistic origin. \n\n### 1. The Scientific Definition\nIn astronomy, a **comet** is an icy, small Solar System body that orbits the Sun. Often described as \"dirty snowballs\" or \"icy dirtballs,\" comets are made of frozen gases (like water, carbon dioxide, and methane), rock, and dust. \n\nUnlike asteroids, which are mostly rock and metal, a comet's composition causes it to change when it gets close to the Sun:\n* **The Coma:** As a comet heats up, the ice on its surface begins to vaporize (sublime), releasing a giant, glowing cloud of gas and dust around its core (called the *nucleus*). This glowing envelope is called the **coma**.\n* **The Tail:** Radiation and solar wind from the Sun push this dust and gas away, forming one or more spectacular, glowing **tails** that always point away from the Sun.\n\n### 2. The Word Origin (Etymology)\nThe literal meaning of the word *comet* is **\"long-haired.\"**\n\n* **Ancient Greek:** Around 500 BC, Ancient Greek philosophers observed these streaking objects in the sky and called them ***kom\u0113t\u0113s* (\u03ba\u03bf\u03bc\u03ae\u03c4\u03b7\u03c2)**, which translates to \"wearing long hair\" or \"long-haired\".\n* **The \"Hairy Star\":** This was short for the phrase ***ast\u0113r kom\u0113t\u0113s* (\u1f00\u03c3\u03c4\u1f74\u03c1 \u03ba\u03bf\u03bc\u03ae\u03c4\u03b7\u03c2)**, meaning **\"long-haired star\"**\u2014because the wispy, glowing tail of a comet looked to them like long, flowing hair trailing behind a head.\n* **Evolution of the Word:** The Greek word was borrowed into Latin as *cometa*, which made its way into Old French as *comete*, and finally entered the English language around the 12th century. \n\n*(Fun fact: In medieval England, comets were sometimes literally referred to as \"faxed stars,\" with \"fax\" being an Old English word for a head of hair).*",
